	fmt.Println("Vectors with metadata prepared successfully!")
	// In a real application, you'd perform the upsert and then query.
}
How Context is Stored and Used:

Storage: When you upsert the vector, Pinecone stores the original_text, source, category, author_id, timestamp, publish_date, and tags as metadata alongside the numerical embedding.

Filtering during Queries: This is where the power comes in. When you query Pinecone with a vector, you can also apply metadata filters. This allows you to restrict your search to only return results that match specific criteria in their metadata.


